    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{D}.\n\nDuring apheresis, citrate is used as an anticoagulant to prevent clotting in the extracorporeal circuit, which binds to the donor's ionized calcium. The resulting transient hypocalcemia commonly manifests as perioral tingling and numbness. This reaction is a well-known side effect of citrate toxicity and is managed by slowing the procedure or administering calcium supplements.",
