    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Bacteria associated with caries are able to cause the same due to:\n\nOptions: \nA. Production of extxacellular polysaccharides like dextrans and levans\nB. Production of acid to hydrolyse diet carbohydrates\nC. Production of enzymes which inhibit normal flora\nD. Inhibition of secretory IgA\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The primary mechanism by which cariogenic bacteria, such as *Streptococcus mutans*, induce cavities is through the fermentation of dietary carbohydrates into acids, which demineralize tooth enamel. Additionally, these bacteria produce extracellular polysaccharides (like dextrans and levans) from sucrose, which facilitate their adhesion to the tooth surface and form the protective matrix of dental plaque. While other factors may play minor roles, the collective process of acid production and biofilm formation is the hallmark of dental caries.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
