  {
    "global index": "MedMCQA_2477",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following rightly describes the mechanism of \"Pressor Escape\" in SIADH?\n\nOptions: \nA. Characterized by sudden increase in urine volume with decrease in urine osmolality independent of circulating vasopressin levels.\nB. Characterized by sudden increase in urine volume with decrease in urine osmolality dependent of circulating vasopressin levels.\nC. Characterized by sudden decrease in urine volume with increase in urine osmolality independent of circulating vasopressin levels.\nD. Characterized by sudden decrease in urine volume with increase in urine osmolality dependent of circulating vasopressin levels.=\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The term \"Pressor Escape\" (often synonymous with the \"Vasopressin Escape\" phenomenon) describes the mechanism by which the kidney eventually becomes resistant to the antidiuretic effects of high vasopressin levels. This leads to a water diuresis, characterized by an increase in urine volume and a decrease in urine osmolality, despite the continued presence of high circulating vasopressin. Therefore, it is a process that operates independently of any immediate changes in circulating vasopressin levels to restore water balance.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 185,
        "output_tokens": 109,
        "total_tokens": 294
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
