      "generated_answer": "The study found that at 150 msec (minimizing eye movement), there was no difference in speed discrimination between schizophrenia patients and controls, suggesting intact motion perception without eye movement interference. However, at 300 msec (allowing eye movement), patients performed worse, and their performance was not correlated with eye-tracking frequency, indicating that poor motion perception in patients is not due to eye-tracking abnormalities. Thus, motion perception deficits are not simply a consequence of eye-tracking issues.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
