    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4838",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Archaeological evidence for the collapse of civilizations suggests which of the following is the most important variable?\n\nOptions: \nA. the existence of a written language\nB. the number of years the civilization has existed\nC. the civilization's religious beliefs and practices\nD. whether warfare can be ended\nE. the size of the civilization's population\nF. the ability of bureaucracies to store food and feed its people\nG. the civilization's trade networks with other societies\nH. changing environmental conditions\nI. the development of new technologies\nJ. how societies respond to challenges\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most widely accepted archaeological and anthropological perspective, popularized by scholars like Joseph Tainter and Jared Diamond, emphasizes that a civilization's fate rests on its flexibility and organizational capacity when confronting external and internal pressures. While environmental changes and resource management are critical factors, they are ultimately mediated by the society's decision-making process. Therefore, determining how a civilization adapts to these variables is the most significant indicator of its long-term survival.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
