y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    # Parse first line: N, M\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx])\n    M = int(data[idx+1])\n    idx += 2\n    \n    # Initialize graph\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    # Read M edges\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(data[idx])\n        v = int(data[idx+1])\n        idx += 2\n        if u == v:  # self-loop\n            continue\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    # Read K\n    K = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    # Read K forbidden pairs (x_i, y_i)\n    forbidden = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(data[idx])\n        y = int(data[idx+1])\n        idx += 2\n        forbidden.append((x, y))\n    \n    # Read Q\n    Q = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    # Read Q queries (p_i, q_i)\n    queries = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(data[idx])\n        q = int(data[idx+1])\n        idx += 2\n        queries.append((p, q))\n    \n    # Find connected components using BFS/DFS\n    visited = [False] * (N + 1)\n    components = {}\n    \n    comp_id = 0\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        if not visited[i]:\n            comp_id += 1\n            # BFS to find all nodes in this component\n            queue = deque([i])\n            visited[i] = True\n            components[i] = comp_id\n            while queue:\n                node = queue.popleft()\n                for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                    if not visited[neighbor]:\n                        visited[neighbor] = True\n                        components[neighbor] = comp_id\n                        queue.append(neighbor)\n    \n    # For nodes not connected to any edge (isolated), assign component ID\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        if i not in components:\n            comp_id += 1\n            components[i] = comp_id\n    \n    # For each forbidden pair, store the component IDs of x_i and y_i\n    # Since in the original graph, x_i and y_i are not connected, they must be in different components\n    forbidden_component_pairs = []\n    for (x, y) in forbidden:\n        comp_x = components[x]\n        comp_y = components[y]\n        # Ensure ordering for consistency (smaller first)\n        if comp_x > comp_y:\n            comp_x, comp_y = comp_y, comp_x\n        forbidden_component_pairs.append((comp_x, comp_y))\n    \n    # Sort and make unique to avoid redundant checks\n    forbidden_component_pairs = set(forbidden_component_pairs)\n    \n    # Process each query\n    results = []\n    for (p, q) in queries:\n        # Get component IDs for p and q\n        comp_p = components[p]\n        comp_q = components[q]\n        \n        # If they are already in the same component, adding the edge doesn't change connectivity\n        if comp_p == comp_q:\n            # The components stay the same, so all forbidden pairs still have no path\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # They are in different components; adding edge (p,q) will merge comp_p and comp_q\n            # After merging, check if any forbidden pair becomes connected\n            # That is, if a forbidden pair was (x,y) with comp_x = min(comp_p, comp_q) and comp_y = max(comp_p, comp_q),\n            # then after merging they become connected -> violates condition\n            if comp_p > comp_q:\n                comp_p, comp_q = comp_q, comp_p\n            if (comp_p, comp_q) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    # Output each result on a new line\n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\nsolve()\n```",
