    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_6",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAlex is participating in the filming of another video of BrMeast, and BrMeast asked Alex to prepare 250 thousand tons of TNT, but Alex didn't hear him well, so he prepared $n$ boxes and arranged them in a row waiting for trucks. The $i$-th box from the left weighs $a_i$ tons.\n\nAll trucks that Alex is going to use hold the same number of boxes, denoted by $k$. Loading happens the following way:\n\n \n-  The first $k$ boxes goes to the first truck, \n-  The second $k$ boxes goes to the second truck, \n-  $\\dotsb$ \n-  The last $k$ boxes goes to the $\\frac{{n}}{{k}}$-th truck. Upon loading is completed, each truck must have exactly $k$ boxes. In other words, if at some point it is not possible to load exactly $k$ boxes into the truck, then the loading option with that $k$ is not possible.\n\nAlex hates justice, so he wants the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of two trucks to be as great as possible. If there is only one truck, this value is $0$.\n\nAlex has quite a lot of connections, so for every $1 \\leq k \\leq n$, he can find a company such that each of its trucks can hold exactly $k$ boxes. Print the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of any two trucks.\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains one integer $t$ ($1 \\leq t \\leq 10^4$) — the number of test cases.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer $n$ ($1 \\leq n \\leq 150\\,000$) — the number of boxes.\n\nThe second line contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$ ($1 \\leq a_i \\leq 10^9$) — the weights of the boxes.\n\nIt is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ for all test cases does not exceed $150\\,000$.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, print a single integer — the answer to the problem.Sample Input 1:\n5\n\n2\n\n1 2\n\n6\n\n10 2 3 6 1 3\n\n4\n\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\n15\n\n60978 82265 78961 56708 39846 31071 4913 4769 29092 91348 64119 72421 98405 222 14294\n\n8\n\n19957 69913 37531 96991 57838 21008 14207 19198\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\n1\n9\n0\n189114\n112141\n\n\nNote\n\nIn the first case, we should pick two trucks, so the first one will have only the first box, and the second one will have only the second box.\n\nIn the second case, we should pick six trucks, so the maximum will be $10$, the minimum will be $1$, and the answer is $10 - 1 = 9$.\n\nIn the third case, for any possible $k$, the trucks will have the same total weight of boxes, so the answer is $0$.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are tasked with solving the following problem:\n\nFor each test case:\n- We are given `n` boxes with weights `a[i]`.\n- For every possible `k` such that `k` divides `n` (i.e., `n % k == 0`), we can partition the boxes into `n // k` trucks, each carrying `k` consecutive boxes.\n- The weight of a truck is the sum of the `k` boxes assigned to it.\n- For such a `k`, compute the **maximum absolute difference** between any two truck weights (i.e., `max_sum - min_sum`).\n- We want the **maximum** of this difference **over all valid divisors `k` of `n`**.\n\nIf only one truck exists (i.e., `k = n`), then the difference is 0.\n\nWe are to compute this maximum possible difference across all valid `k`.\n\n---\n\n### Approach:\n1. For each test case, iterate over all divisors `k` of `n`.\n2. For each divisor `k`, split the array into `n // k` groups of `k` consecutive elements.\n3. Compute the sum of each group.\n4. Compute the difference `max(group_sums) - min(group_sums)`.\n5. Update the answer to be the maximum such difference over all `k`.\n6. Print the result.\n\n---\n\n### Optimization Note:\n- The total sum of `n` across all test cases ≤ 150,000.\n- However, we must be careful: for each `n`, we need to iterate over all divisors and simulate grouping.\n- But note: the number of divisors of `n` is small (around O(√n)), and the sum of work over all divisors across test cases is acceptable, given the constraint on total `n` ≤ 150,000.\n\nWe can safely do:\n```python\nfor k in divisors(n):\n    if n % k == 0:\n        groups = n // k\n        # split array into groups of size k\n```\n\n---\n\n### Implementation:\n\nWe'll do:\n- Read number of test cases.\n- For each test case:\n  - Read `n`\n  - Read list `a` of `n` integers\n  - Find all divisors of `n`\n  - For each divisor `k`:\n    - Split `a` into chunks of size `k`\n    - Compute min and max of chunk sums\n    - Update answer = max(answer, max_sum - min_sum)\n  - Print answer\n\n---\n\nLet’s write the code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom math import isqrt\n\ndef get_divisors(n):\n    divs = []\n    for i in range(1, isqrt(n) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divs.append(i)\n            if i != n // i:\n                divs.append(n // i)\n    return divs\n\ndef solve():\n    n = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n    a = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n    \n    divisors = get_divisors(n)\n    best_diff = 0\n    \n    for k in divisors:\n        if n % k != 0:\n            continue\n        num_trucks = n // k\n        group_sums = []\n        for i in range(0, n, k):\n            group_sum = sum(a[i:i+k])\n            group_sums.append(group_sum)\n        \n        if len(group_sums) == 1:\n            diff = 0\n        else:\n            diff = max(group_sums) - min(group_sums)\n        if diff > best_diff:\n            best_diff = diff\n    \n    print(best_diff)\n\nt = int(sys.stdin.readline())\nfor _ in range(t):\n    solve()\n```\n\nThis should handle the constraints efficiently.\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of Sample:\n**Test 1:** `n=2`, `a=[1,2]`\n- Divisors: 1, 2\n- k=1: groups = [1],[2] → sums = [1,2] → diff = 1\n- k=2: groups = [1,2] → sums = [3] → diff = 0\n- Max = 1 ✅\n\n**Test 2:** `n=6`, `a=[10,2,3,6,1,3]`\n- Try k=6: one truck → diff=0\n- k=3: two trucks: [10+2+3=15], [6+1+3=10] → diff=5\n- k=2: three trucks: [10+2=12], [3+6=9], [1+3=4] → diff = 12–4 = 8\n- k=1: six trucks: [10],[2],[3],[6],[1],[3] → max=10, min=1 → diff=9\n- Max = 9 ✅\n\nSo the code is correct.\n\nLet’s run with the given input structure.\n\nFinal code below:\n\n---\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom math import isqrt\n\ndef get_divisors(n):\n    divs = []\n    for i in range(1, isqrt(n) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divs.append(i)\n            if i != n // i:\n                divs.append(n // i)\n    return divs\n\ndef solve():\n    n = int(sys.stdin.readline())\n    a = list(map(int, sys.stdin.readline().split()))\n    \n    divisors = get_divisors(n)\n    best_diff = 0\n    \n    for k in divisors:\n        if n % k != 0:\n            continue\n        group_sums = []\n        for i in range(0, n, k):\n            total = sum(a[i:i+k])\n            group_sums.append(total)\n        \n        if len(group_sums) == 1:\n            diff = 0\n        else:\n            diff = max(group_sums) - min(group_sums)\n        if diff > best_diff:\n            best_diff = diff\n    \n    print(best_diff)\n\nt = int(sys.stdin.readline())\nfor _ in range(t):\n    solve()\n```",
