      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step-by-step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We need to find all subsequences of length `k` from the array `nums`\n2. For each subsequence, calculate its \"power\" which is the minimum absolute difference between any two elements\n3. Sum up the powers of all such subsequences and return the result modulo 10^9 + 7\n\n## Key Insights\n\n1. Since we're dealing with absolute differences, the order of elements in a subsequence doesn't matter for the power calculation\n2. To efficiently calculate the minimum absolute difference in a subsequence, it's helpful to sort the elements first\n3. The minimum absolute difference in a sorted subsequence will be the minimum difference between adjacent elements\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Sort the array first to make it easier to work with subsequences\n2. Generate all possible subsequences of length `k`\n3. For each subsequence, sort it (though we'll leverage the fact we sorted the original array)\n4. Calculate the minimum absolute difference between adjacent elements in the sorted subsequence\n5. Sum all these minimum differences\n\n## Step-by-Step Solution\n\n### Step 1: Sort the array\nThis makes it easier to work with subsequences and calculate minimum differences.\n\n### Step 2: Generate all subsequences of length k\nWe can use backtracking or combinations to generate all possible subsequences.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate power for each subsequence\nFor each subsequence, find the minimum absolute difference between any two elements. Since the original array is sorted, when we pick elements by their indices, we can sort the picked elements and check adjacent differences.\n\n### Step 4: Sum all powers\nKeep a running sum and apply modulo operation.\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()  # Sort to make it easier to work with\n    \n    def get_power(subsequence):\n        # Get the minimum absolute difference in the subsequence\n        if len(subsequence) < 2:\n            return 0\n        # Sort the subsequence to find minimum adjacent difference\n        sorted_sub = sorted(subsequence)\n        min_diff = float('inf')\n        for i in range(len(sorted_sub) - 1):\n            min_diff = min(min_diff, abs(sorted_sub[i + 1] - sorted_sub[i]))\n        return min_diff\n    \n    def backtrack(start, current_sub):\n        # Base case: we have a subsequence of length k\n        if len(current_sub) == k:\n            return get_power(current_sub)\n        \n        # If we can't form a subsequence of length k from remaining elements\n        if start + (k - len(current_sub)) > n:\n            return 0\n        \n        total_power = 0\n        # Include current element and recurse\n        for i in range(start, n):\n            current_sub.append(nums[i])\n            total_power = (total_power + backtrack(i + 1, current_sub)) % MOD\n            current_sub.pop()  # backtrack\n        \n        return total_power % MOD\n    \n    return backtrack(0, [])\n```",
