       for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if not possible[i][j]:\n                    continue\n                # The cell (i, j) is reachable after forward moves processed so far.\n                # In the reverse direction, it came from (i+di, j+dj)\n                ni = i + di\n                nj = j + dj\n                if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '.':\n                    new_possible[ni][nj] = True\n        possible = new_possible\n\n    # After processing all reversed moves, possible[i][j] is True\n    # if (i,j) could be the crash-landing cell (starting cell).\n    # The question asks for the number of possible *current* positions.\n    # Wait: we started with possible being all land cells (possible current positions).\n    # Then we went backwards along the moves; after all moves, possible holds\n    # the possible *starting* cells. That's the opposite of what we need.\n    # Actually we want the number of cells that could be his current position\n    # given the moves. The method above counts possible *starting* positions.\n    # We can instead process moves forward from all possible starting positions.\n    # But that would be more expensive? We can instead reverse the logic:\n    # Let's start with all land cells as possible starting positions,\n    # then simulate forward moves. The final possible set is current positions.\n    # Complexity same: O(N * H * W) = 500*250k = 125M, which might be borderline\n    # but H,W <=500, H*W=250k, N=500, 125M checks with simple operations,\n    # plus grid access. Python might be okay with some optimization (using list of bytearray, etc).\n    # However the reverse method already computed starting cells. To get current cells,\n    # we can just apply the forward moves to the starting set? But we lost the starting set\n    # after transformation. Alternative: we can compute forward DP from all land cells\n    # and then count reachable after N moves.\n    # Let's do forward DP: initially possible_start = all land cells.\n    # Then for each move, compute next set.\n    # That gives current positions at the end.\n    # We already have code that computes reversed; we can adapt:\n    # Instead of reversing moves, start from all land cells and apply moves forward.\n\n    # So let's rewrite: use forward propagation.\n    possible = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                possible[i][j] = True\n\n    # Forward move directions:\n    fwd = {'L': (0, -1), 'R': (0, 1), 'U': (-1, 0), 'D': (1, 0)}\n    for ch in T:\n        di, dj = fwd[ch]\n        new_possible = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if not possible[i][j]:\n                    continue\n                ni = i + di\n                nj = j + dj\n                if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '.':\n                    new_possible[ni][nj] = True\n        possible = new_possible\n\n    # Count True cells\n    ans = sum(sum(row) for row in possible)\n    return ans\n\n```",
