      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to minimize |max(nums1) - min(nums2)| when partitioning the array into two non-empty parts.\n\nKey insights:\n1. To minimize the absolute difference, I want max(nums1) and min(nums2) to be as close as possible\n2. Since every element must go to one of the two arrays, if I sort the array, the optimal partition will occur at some point where I split the sorted array\n3. For a sorted array, if I split at position i (0 < i < n), then:\n   - nums1 contains elements from index 0 to i-1, so max(nums1) = sorted[i-1]\n   - nums2 contains elements from index i to n-1, so min(nums2) = sorted[i]\n   - The partition value is |sorted[i-1] - sorted[i]| = sorted[i] - sorted[i-1] (since sorted[i] >= sorted[i-1])\n\nThe minimum possible value will be the minimum difference between consecutive elements in the sorted array.\n\n```python\ndef findValueOfPartition(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    \n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        diff = nums[i] - nums[i-1]\n        min_diff = min(min_diff, diff)\n    \n    return min_diff\n```",
